94 Devstral 2 followed the MCP recipe cleanly and built a fully working, correctly modeled block-grid blog (3 blocks, adapter + ViewComponent pipeline, domain, imported Unsplash media) with every automated check passing. The main weaknesses are thin, generic post copy and an image component view that depends on Umbraco's MediaWithCrops.
works 35 / 35 : Build OK (the 10 warnings are the scaffold's ImageSharp NuGet advisories, none introduced). / is the blog front page through the '/' domain on the Blog page; it lists both posts as cards with image, title, date, excerpt and Read more link. Both posts render all 4 blocks (rich text, image, rich text, quote) with no render errors. 4 provided Unsplash photos were imported into a media folder and served via GetCropUrl; 6/6 images load. All content is published.
modeling 25 / 25 : richTextBlock/imageBlock/quoteBlock are elements in Blocks; blogFrontPage/blogPost are documents in Pages. 'Blog Block Grid' in Custom/Umbraco.BlockGrid allows all three blocks and is used for body on both page types. siteRoot is the only root type, siteRoot→blogFrontPage→blogPost, templates assigned, camelCase aliases. Post metadata (publishDate, excerpt, listingImage) are real properties, there are no compositions, and nothing is duplicated.
architecture 23 / 25 : Pages use Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "body"), and the scaffold's grid partials are unchanged. There is one markup-free adapter per block at blockgrid/Components/<alias>.cshtml, each calling Component.InvokeAsync, and one ViewComponent per block with Default.cshtml views and plain models in Models/Blocks/. -2: ImageBlockModel exposes Umbraco's MediaWithCrops, and ImageBlock/Default.cshtml calls GetCropUrl, so the component view depends on Umbraco (the image URL should be built in the adapter). Program.cs and appsettings.json are untouched, there are no migrations, friendly extensions are used, and styling goes through the Tailwind build.
content 6 / 10 : Content 3/6: both posts are on topic and spread over 4 blocks with relevant photos (cables, code), but they are thin, about 4 short paragraphs (~120 words) each, generic, with made-up anonymous quote attributions ('Tech Industry Analyst', 'AI Researcher'). Presentation 3/4: a clean Tailwind blog with a header, hero, card grid, post card and footer. But rich text is wrapped in a 'prose' class that doesn't exist (no typography plugin), so paragraphs have no spacing. The front page's rich text block also sits loose and unstyled below the listing.
process 5 / 5 : Schema, media, content and the domain were all built through the umbraco/umbraco-editor MCP servers (44 calls). The recipe's step order was followed: model → media → content → publish → domain → stop/build → Razor → npm build → rebuild/restart → curl checks. No forbidden channels, no memory access, no outside-folder access. The final report is accurate and verified with curl.
[
 {
  "severity": "major",
  "text": "Posts are thin: about 4 short paragraphs (~120 words) each, generic claims and invented anonymous quotes (/mcp-connects-ai-to-applications/, /mcp-powers-the-future-of-ai/)"
 },
 {
  "severity": "minor",
  "text": "ImageBlockModel (Models/Blocks/ImageBlockModel.cs) carries MediaWithCrops, and Views/Shared/Components/ImageBlock/Default.cshtml calls GetCropUrl, so the component view depends on Umbraco"
 },
 {
  "severity": "minor",
  "text": "Rich text uses a 'prose' class that isn't in site.css (no typography plugin), so paragraphs render with no spacing (Views/Shared/Components/RichText/Default.cshtml)"
 },
 {
  "severity": "minor",
  "text": "The front page's rich text block renders below the post grid without styling or a heading (Views/blogFrontPage.cshtml)"
 }
]
----
95.5 Devstral 2 followed the MCP recipe cleanly and built a fully working, correctly modeled block-grid blog: 3 blocks, the adapter → ViewComponent pipeline with Umbraco-free component views, a / domain, and imported Unsplash media. Every automated check passes. The weak spots are the thin, generic post copy and a plain layout with unspaced rich-text paragraphs.
works 35 / 35 : Build OK: 0 errors. The 10 warnings are the scaffold's ImageSharp NuGet advisories, and the model added none. / returns 200 through the '/' domain on the Blog page. It lists both posts as cards with a listing image, title, date, excerpt and Read more link, and the links work. Both posts render all 4 blocks (rich text, image, rich text, quote) with no render errors. The 4 provided Unsplash photos were imported into a 'Blog Images' media folder and are served through GetCropUrl, and 4/4 images load. All content is published.
modeling 25 / 25 : richTextBlock, imageBlock and quoteBlock are element types in Blocks. blogFrontPage and blogPost are document types in Pages. 'Blog Block Grid' sits in Custom/Umbraco.BlockGrid, allows all three blocks, and is the body property on both page types. siteRoot is the only type allowed at root. The tree is siteRoot → blogFrontPage → blogPost, templates are assigned, and aliases are camelCase. publishDate, excerpt and listingImage are real properties. There are no compositions and no duplicated or orphaned types.
architecture 25 / 25 : Both templates use Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "body"), and the scaffold's grid partials are unchanged. There is one markup-free adapter per block at blockgrid/Components/<alias>.cshtml. Each maps BlockGridItem<T> to a plain model in Models/Blocks/ (it builds the GetCropUrl in the adapter) and calls Component.InvokeAsync. There is one ViewComponent per block, and the Default.cshtml views have no Umbraco dependencies. Program.cs and appsettings.json are untouched, there are no migrations, and styling goes through the Tailwind build. Nits, not deducted: both templates repeat the full HTML shell instead of sharing a layout, and the Back link is hard-coded to /.
content 5.5 / 10 : Content 3/6: both posts are on topic and spread over 4 blocks with relevant photos, but they are thin and generic. Each has about 4 short paragraphs (~110 words) and an invented anonymous quote ('Tech Enthusiast', 'Developer Advocate'). Part 2 says it will 'dive deeper into the technical aspects' but stays vague. Presentation 2.5/4: a clean, readable single-column Tailwind layout with proper listing cards and a tidy post page. It has no site header, nav or footer. Rich text is wrapped in a 'prose' class that does nothing without the typography plugin, so paragraphs run together with no spacing. The front page's rich text block is a single placeholder sentence.
process 5 / 5 : Schema, media, content, publishing and the domain all went through the umbraco and umbraco-editor MCP servers (41 calls). It followed the recipe's order: schema → media → content → publish → domain → stop/build → Razor → npm build → rebuild/restart → curl checks. It used no forbidden channels, did not access memory, and stayed in the project folder. Its only read outside the folder was its own background-task output. The final report is accurate and was verified with curl.
